    "knowledge_point_id": "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point",
    "problem": "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-10,\\,y=4{t}^{6}+5.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12{t}^{4},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$",
    "worked_solution": "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=12{t}^{4},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-10)=2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(12{t}^{4})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{48{t}^{3}}{2t} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=24\\cdot {1}^{2}=24.\n$$",
